From 59a84c4e485634f32b6fdbaba05789bf7b445ef2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vincent <vincent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 08:46:41 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] gateway: a tool call answered by no result stops after 8
 turns (#3615)

easonlao (#3615): zcode on Windows made 1088 requests to
group/auto-kimi-k3 in 5h08m, every one answered 200 with
stop=tool_calls and a ~150-token tool call, the prompt growing from
139,956 to 431,953 tokens. The client never sent a tool result back, so
every turn was the same call answered by nothing, and magpie counted
none of them as a loop.

The tool loop guard only read turns that have results: exchanges and
exchangeOf (internal/gateway/tool_loop.go) break at a turn without one,
and mayLoop (internal/gateway/tool_loop.go:74) parsed only a body
holding 8 tool results. A conversation of the same call with nothing
answering it was therefore never read.

stoppedToolLoop now also asks toolLoopUnansweredOf
(internal/gateway/tool_loop.go). It walks the request's own messages
back from the newest and counts the calls asked in a row with no result
after them, the same name and arguments (key order ignored, call ids
ignored). Eight in a row answer the turn here, in the agent's own
protocol, with a notice saying the result never came back, so nothing
claims whether the call ran, and the turn goes nowhere. A turn asking a
different call, a reply that says something else, a result, or a word
from the user starts the count again, as the result-shaped check does.
mayLoop reads a body with 8 tool calls as well as 8 tool results
(toolCallMarks, matched loosely: a mark that also matches a result only
costs a parse). No new setting and no GUI string: Settings' Stop
looping replies turns it off with the reply guard, and the usage row is
the same "tool loop:" rejection the result-shaped check writes.

Every protocol's path runs through stoppedToolLoop, so the Codex
backend's passthrough is covered by the same change, and each API is
tested: Responses (stream and whole), Codex's own path, Chat (stream
and whole), Anthropic (stream and whole) and Gemini.

Run at this commit:
- gofmt -l internal/gateway/tool_loop.go internal/gateway/tool_loop_test.go: nothing.
- go build ./...: nothing.
- go vet ./internal/gateway/: nothing.
- go test -tags nogui ./internal/gateway/ -run 'TestToolLoop' -count=1: ok,
  39 tests pass. TestToolLoopStuckWithoutResults stops 8 answerless turns
  of one call on all eight protocol cases with the vendor asked 0 times
  and the usage row magpie, rejected, "tool loop ... no result", and sends
  7 such turns on; TestToolLoopStuckNeedsTheSameCallAndNoWord lets
  differing calls and a word from the user between the calls through.
- With tool_loop.go reverted and the test kept, the same test fails on all
  eight cases: "the agent wasn't told of the loop in its API", the
  vendor's reply reaching the agent.
- go test -tags nogui ./internal/gateway/ -count=1 was run once and failed
  only in five tests that fail on clean upstream/main too: with every change
  here reverted (git diff saved and applied back, no stash), the same five
  fail in the same way (TestClaudeClientCacheAbsentUnchanged,
  TestCodexFittedImagesPastTheBudget, TestMarshalRawIsEncodingJSON and
  FuzzMarshalRaw's \u2028 escaping, TestServeWSLListensBesideLoopback).
  A second full-suite run at this commit was not completed.

Not tried:
- A real zcode session: the reporter's 1088 routing entries are not
  available, so the fixture is the shape its numbers describe (one call a
  turn, no result, the prompt growing by the call alone).
- The issue's other option, a per-session windowed cap or a refusal: it
  needs a new setting and session state, and is left to the maintainer.
- The Windows box.
